{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790327393,"nonce":"uPCw33DYJJwB+RZPb3LNRnZPf1U=","title":"Zyntax languages use other languages' classes and plugins: host objects","message":"Python and Lua under Zyntax should import a Haxe or Wren class or a plugin\nand use it (the HXSL shader from Python and Lua is the test). Today they\ncannot: zyntax_python's resolver returns Python source only, zyntax_lua's\nrequire reads .lua files and package.preload only, neither has a host\nobject, and the adapter passes scalars and strings only.\n\nShape, as the user chose on 2026-09-25: Python sees a host class as a typed\nextern class; Lua sees host objects dynamically. The Zyntax side is done on\na branch in a zyntax worktree, not in the owner's tree, and handed over to\nmerge.\n\nZyntax (branch caribou/host-objects):\n- zyntax_builtins: a host-object kind in the custom category, its payload an\n  opaque handle, and host-protocol calls the embedder implements: get\n  member, set member, call, invoke method, to string, equality, release\n  (called when the box is freed).\n- zyntax_lua: index, newindex, call, tostring and == of a host object go\n  through the protocol; require asks the host for a module it does not find\n  as a file, e.g. require(\"haxe:ScaleValues\").\n- zyntax_python: the resolver can answer with a host module's typed\n  declarations (classes, their fields, methods and constructors; module\n  functions). Python checks calls against them and lowers them to the\n  protocol, boxing and unboxing at the declared types.\n\nCaribou:\n- caribou-lua: Lua as a language of the world over zyntax_lua.\n- caribou-zyntax: the protocol over the core's (bridge get/set/invoke/call),\n  host handles rooted in the core and released by the protocol, Value to box\n  and back (scalars, strings, host objects, and a Zyntax value crossing back\n  out as what it was).\n- Answer module requests from the registry: a Lua host module, and Python's\n  typed declarations from the published Interface.\n\nTest: Python and Lua modules import the HXSL compute shader class from the\ngpu fixture's Haxe program and run it through the gpu plugin.","files":null}]}